item.text:
Searches covered nuclear posture and command, Taiwan crisis management, DPRK sanctions implementation, military-AI practice, nuclear safeguards/forensics, and CTBT status. The strongest recoveries are the Pentagon’s posture assessment, the USCC’s ruler-attribution analysis, a contemporaneous account of Xi’s de-escalatory message, UN sanctions-monitoring findings, and two independent studies contradicting China’s military-AI assurances. The evidence distinguishes reported conduct from Chinese denials and does not convert private-party activity into Xi-directed assistance. Relevant underlying sources include the [2022 Pentagon report](https://media.defense.gov/2022/Nov/29/2003122279/-1/-1/1/2022-MILITARY-AND-SECURITY-DEVELOPMENTS-INVOLVING-THE-PEOPLES-REPUBLIC-OF-CHINA.PDF), [2022 USCC report](https://www.uscc.gov/sites/default/files/2022-11/2022_Annual_Report_to_Congress.pdf), [UN DPRK Panel report S/2023/171](https://documents.un.org/api/symbol/access?l=en&s=S%2F2023%2F171&t=pdf), and peer-reviewed military-AI studies in the [Chinese Journal of International Politics](https://academic.oup.com/cjip/article/16/1/106/6976053) and [Frontiers in Big Data](https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/big-data/articles/10.3389/fdata.2022.991392/full).

Still unresolved: public evidence of 2022 nuclear accidents or military-warhead inspections; actual nuclear/dual-use appointment criteria and independent adviser dissent; direct documentary proof that Xi personally approved the detailed Taiwan exercise plan; a demonstrated operational “nuclear cover” linkage; and audited, system-wide implementation of military-AI, biological, or cyber catastrophic-risk safeguards. Public sources support strong Xi authority but leave opaque whether and how he exercised it in individual technical decisions.

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Military and Security Developments Involving the People’s Republic of China 2022","publisher":"United States Department of Defense","publication_date":"2022-11-29","url":"https://media.defense.gov/2022/Nov/29/2003122279/-1/-1/1/2022-MILITARY-AND-SECURITY-DEVELOPMENTS-INVOLVING-THE-PEOPLES-REPUBLIC-OF-CHINA.PDF","claim":"The Pentagon assessed that China probably kept most nuclear forces in peacetime with launchers, missiles, and warheads separated, but was implementing an early-warning counterstrike posture comparable to launch on warning; readiness measures reportedly included rotating a missile battalion onto standby. This is an intelligence assessment, not a confirmed Chinese disclosure.","locator":"PDF pp. 96–97, subsection \"Nuclear Readiness\" and discussion of early-warning counterstrike/launch on warning","provisional_id":"WEB-1B-C1-001","canonical_fact_key":"xi-2022-nuclear-readiness-low-transition","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["1B"],"methodology_ids":["1B.3"],"source_type":"official_intelligence_assessment","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Statutorily required contemporary defense assessment with explicit uncertainty language and operational detail, though based partly on classified intelligence that cannot be independently inspected.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Published in 2022 and assesses developments through 2021/2022; directly applicable to the inherited posture and ongoing transition during Xi’s 2022 rule.","ruler_attribution":"Xi was CMC chairman and apex military authority, but the source establishes system-level posture rather than a documented personal order for each readiness measure.","contrary_evidence":true,"underlying_fact_key":"china-2022-nuclear-readiness-and-launch-warning","lenses":["1B.3"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"2022 Annual Report to Congress","publisher":"U.S.-China Economic and Security Review Commission","publication_date":"2022-11-15","url":"https://www.uscc.gov/sites/default/files/2022-11/2022_Annual_Report_to_Congress.pdf","claim":"The Commission concluded that nuclear command had become highly centralized and personalized under Xi. Expert testimony warned that, if China adopted launch on warning, Xi could have roughly ten minutes to make the personal nuclear-use decision; the report simultaneously stated that China did not yet have such a posture, although its capabilities could enable one.","locator":"PDF pp. 92–93, \"Consequences for Security Decision-Making under Xi,\" especially p. 93 and the launch-on-warning footnote","provisional_id":"WEB-1B-C1-002","canonical_fact_key":"xi-personalized-nuclear-command-2022","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["1B"],"methodology_ids":["1B.1","1B.3","1B.7"],"source_type":"legislative_commission_assessment","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporary bipartisan congressional commission synthesis that identifies its expert-testimony basis and distinguishes actual posture from contingent risk.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Published during calendar 2022 and specifically evaluates Xi-era decision-making structures then in force.","ruler_attribution":"Direct: the assessment identifies Xi as the final, personalized decision authority, while presenting the ten-minute scenario as contingent rather than observed conduct.","contrary_evidence":true,"underlying_fact_key":"china-2022-nuclear-command-personalization","lenses":["1B.1","1B.3","1B.7"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"2022 Annual Report to Congress","publisher":"U.S.-China Economic and Security Review Commission","publication_date":"2022-11-15","url":"https://www.uscc.gov/sites/default/files/2022-11/2022_Annual_Report_to_Congress.pdf","claim":"The Commission found that Xi’s concentration of final authority reduced lower-level officials’ ability to adapt during crises. It reported that the defense minister could transmit messages to Xi in normal conditions but might not be a reliable channel during a crisis, because decisions and messages converge personally at Xi.","locator":"PDF p. 93, \"Consequences for Security Decision-Making under Xi,\" including the footnote beginning \"Specifically, personalization of command under Xi\"","provisional_id":"WEB-1B-C1-003","canonical_fact_key":"xi-crisis-channel-autonomy-constraints-2022","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["1B"],"methodology_ids":["1B.7","1B.8"],"source_type":"legislative_commission_assessment","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Named-expert evidence synthesized in a contemporary congressional commission report; the limits are expressed as institutional risk rather than a proven failed message in one event.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Contemporary 2022 assessment of the crisis-management system operating during the Taiwan episode.","ruler_attribution":"Direct structural attribution to Xi’s final decision authority and personalization of command.","contrary_evidence":true,"underlying_fact_key":"china-2022-crisis-communication-centralization","lenses":["1B.7","1B.8"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Xi Sought to Send Message to Biden on Taiwan: Now Is No Time for a Crisis","publisher":"The Wall Street Journal","publication_date":"2022-08-11","url":"https://www.wsj.com/articles/xi-sought-to-send-message-to-biden-on-taiwan-now-is-no-time-for-a-crisis-11660240698","claim":"People described as close to China’s decision-making told the Journal that, in the July 28 call before Pelosi’s visit, Xi warned Biden on Taiwan but also indicated that he did not intend to go to war with the United States and that both sides should maintain peace and security. This supplies attributable contrary evidence of a de-escalatory message, not proof that Xi restrained the subsequent exercises.","locator":"Opening paragraphs and section describing the July 28, 2022 Biden–Xi call; headline and subheadline identify the reported finding and sourcing","provisional_id":"WEB-1B-C1-004","canonical_fact_key":"xi-july-2022-taiwan-deescalatory-message","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["1B"],"methodology_ids":["1B.5","1B.8"],"source_type":"independent_investigative_reporting","source_confidence":"medium","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous major-news reporting with sources close to the decision process, but the sources are unnamed and the exact call record is unavailable.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly describes Xi’s conduct on July 28, 2022, immediately before the August Taiwan crisis.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributed to Xi’s own communication with Biden, subject to anonymous-source limitations.","contrary_evidence":true,"underlying_fact_key":"taiwan-crisis-2022-xi-signaling","lenses":["1B.5","1B.8"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"How Beijing Approaches Crisis Management","publisher":"Center for Strategic and International Studies, Interpret: China","publication_date":"2022-11-14","url":"https://interpret.csis.org/how-beijing-approaches-crisis-management/","claim":"Independent analysis found that Beijing had shown little interest in Biden-administration crisis-management discussions and treated agreed mechanisms as politically disposable; after Pelosi’s visit it cancelled three defense dialogues used for maritime rules and crisis management. This weighs against treating Xi’s pre-crisis rhetoric as evidence of durable safeguards.","locator":"Introductory section, paragraph beginning \"These recommendations are well-intentioned,\" including the sentence on cancellation of three defense dialogues in August 2022","provisional_id":"WEB-1B-C1-005","canonical_fact_key":"china-cancelled-crisis-dialogues-august-2022","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["1B"],"methodology_ids":["1B.8"],"source_type":"independent_policy_analysis","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporary specialist analysis tied to publicly announced dialogue cancellations; interpretation is clearly separated from the underlying event.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly evaluates decisions taken during the August 2022 Taiwan crisis.","ruler_attribution":"The cancellations were state decisions under Xi’s centralized foreign-security system, but the source does not document his personal instruction.","contrary_evidence":true,"underlying_fact_key":"china-cancelled-crisis-dialogues-august-2022","lenses":["1B.8"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Final report of the Panel of Experts submitted pursuant to resolution 2627 (2022)","publisher":"United Nations Security Council","publication_date":"2023-03-07","url":"https://documents.un.org/api/symbol/access?l=en&s=S%2F2023%2F171&t=pdf","claim":"A Member State supplied satellite imagery showing three vessels—including China-flagged Peng Lu 9—arriving at Nampo in May 2022 laden with trucks and other items after activity in Chinese ports or waters. China told the Panel that it found no trucks in the DPRK vessels’ deliveries and no evidence that Peng Lu 9 smuggled trucks. The record establishes a documented allegation, investigation, and Chinese denial, not a Panel finding that Xi or the Chinese state supplied the vehicles.","locator":"Paragraphs 90–91 and annex 46, especially figure 46-1 (Nampo, 1 May 2022) and the Chinese response reproduced in paragraph 90","provisional_id":"WEB-1B-C1-006","canonical_fact_key":"dprk-truck-shipments-china-linked-may-2022","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["1B"],"methodology_ids":["1B.6"],"source_type":"un_sanctions_monitoring_report","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UN expert investigation using satellite imagery, maritime databases, formal information requests, and China’s response; attribution limits are explicit.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Later report specifically documenting voyages and cargo observed in May 2022.","ruler_attribution":"No personal Xi direction established; relevant as enforcement practice occurring under agencies ultimately subordinate to his government.","contrary_evidence":true,"underlying_fact_key":"dprk-truck-shipments-china-linked-may-2022","lenses":["1B.6"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Final report of the Panel of Experts submitted pursuant to resolution 2627 (2022)","publisher":"United Nations Security Council","publication_date":"2023-03-07","url":"https://documents.un.org/api/symbol/access?l=en&s=S%2F2023%2F171&t=pdf","claim":"Only 21.06 percent of the UN-permitted 500,000-barrel refined-petroleum cap for 2022 had been officially reported by the end of January 2023. Separately, 52 states documented 45 tanker deliveries to Nampo from January through August and estimated that, depending on loading assumptions, they might have carried 264,127.5–792,382.5 barrels. China said it strictly implemented the reporting and export rules. The discrepancy raises an enforcement question but does not identify China as the source of all unreported fuel.","locator":"Paragraphs 32–33 and figure VI, pp. 27–28; annex 27 for the three loading scenarios","provisional_id":"WEB-1B-C1-007","canonical_fact_key":"dprk-refined-petroleum-reporting-gap-2022","disposition":"context","chapter_ids":["1B"],"methodology_ids":["1B.6"],"source_type":"un_sanctions_monitoring_report","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UN Panel comparison of mandatory reports, satellite-observed deliveries, and formal Chinese representations; uncertainty in cargo loading is quantified.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Later report expressly measures January–November 2022 reporting and January–August 2022 deliveries.","ruler_attribution":"No evidence of Xi ordering material assistance; the fact bears only on national enforcement and transparency under his administration.","contrary_evidence":true,"underlying_fact_key":"dprk-refined-petroleum-reporting-gap-2022","lenses":["1B.6"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Weaponised Artificial Intelligence and Chinese Practices of Human–Machine Interaction","publisher":"The Chinese Journal of International Politics, Oxford University Press","publication_date":"2023-01-09","url":"https://academic.oup.com/cjip/article/16/1/106/6976053","claim":"Peer-reviewed practice-based research found China’s position on lethal autonomous weapons internally ambivalent: it supported a ban while defining covered systems narrowly, had tested or deployed robotic and unmanned systems across all military domains, and was developing increasingly autonomous operational features. The authors concluded these practices could normalize diminished human control despite diplomatic assurances.","locator":"Abstract and HTML paragraphs 65–74; section \"Operational Practices: Designing and Operating Weapons Systems with Autonomous Features,\" paragraphs 175–192","provisional_id":"WEB-1B-C1-008","canonical_fact_key":"china-military-ai-practice-policy-gap-2022","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["1B"],"methodology_ids":["1B.9"],"source_type":"peer_reviewed_scholarship","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Peer-reviewed scholarship examining observable practices and primary Chinese materials; it carefully notes that technical parameters remain opaque.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Accepted in 2022 and published immediately afterward; evaluates practices and policy operating through 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Xi’s modernization and military-civil-fusion agenda supplied top-level direction, but individual platform design and deployment are treated as institutional conduct rather than personal decisions.","contrary_evidence":true,"underlying_fact_key":"china-military-ai-practice-policy-gap-2022","lenses":["1B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"The PRC considers military AI ethics: Can autonomy be trusted?","publisher":"Frontiers in Big Data","publication_date":"2022-10-25","url":"https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/big-data/articles/10.3389/fdata.2022.991392/full","claim":"A peer-reviewed review of PLA and government writings found no publicly available official PLA military-AI ethics policy. It found active internal discussion of accountability, mistaken attacks, loss of control, hacking, and autonomous behavior, but described the published debate as indirect, politically constrained, and unresolved rather than evidence of an implemented testing or oversight regime.","locator":"HTML paragraphs 253–264; sections \"The PLA and military AI ethics\" and \"Summary,\" especially paragraphs 294–325 and 336–344","provisional_id":"WEB-1B-C1-009","canonical_fact_key":"pla-ai-safeguards-public-implementation-gap-2022","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["1B"],"methodology_ids":["1B.7","1B.9"],"source_type":"peer_reviewed_scholarship","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporary peer-reviewed synthesis of named PLA sources that explicitly distinguishes ethical discussion from authoritative policy and implementation.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Published in October 2022 and assesses the public PLA record available during Xi’s 2022 rule.","ruler_attribution":"The study links military ethics to Party control and Xi Jinping Thought, but does not establish Xi’s involvement in particular technical safeguards.","contrary_evidence":true,"underlying_fact_key":"pla-ai-safeguards-public-implementation-gap-2022","lenses":["1B.7","1B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Nuclear Forensics Capability Development for Nuclear Security in China","publisher":"International Atomic Energy Agency Conference Portal","publication_date":"2022-06-20","url":"https://conferences.iaea.org/event/262/contributions/21130/","claim":"A Chinese technical contribution to an IAEA conference described operational nuclear-forensics institutions and IAEA cooperation but acknowledged that China lacked a single sufficiently authoritative and professional body to conduct the entire forensic process and had not completed a comprehensive radioactive-material database; it recommended greater openness and researcher access.","locator":"Sections 2.1–2.3 and 4.1–4.3, especially HTML lines/paragraphs 80–99","provisional_id":"WEB-1B-C1-010","canonical_fact_key":"china-nuclear-forensics-capability-gaps-2022","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["1B"],"methodology_ids":["1B.3","1B.7","1B.9"],"source_type":"technical_conference_record","source_confidence":"medium","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous, unusually candid technical self-assessment hosted by the IAEA, but not an independent audit or formal IAEA finding.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Presented at an IAEA conference in June 2022 and describes capability then in place.","ruler_attribution":"Institutional implementation gap under Xi’s administration; no evidence that Xi personally selected personnel or obstructed the recommended reforms.","contrary_evidence":true,"underlying_fact_key":"china-nuclear-forensics-capability-gaps-2022","lenses":["1B.3","1B.7","1B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Safeguards Statement for 2022","publisher":"International Atomic Energy Agency","publication_date":"2023-06-12","url":"https://www.iaea.org/sites/default/files/23/06/20230612_sir_2022_part_ab.pdf","claim":"The IAEA reported that safeguards were implemented during 2022 at ten selected facilities across the five nuclear-weapon states, including China, under voluntary-offer agreements. This verifies some safeguards activity but does not support a conclusion that China’s military nuclear inventory or all civilian facilities were subject to comprehensive safeguards.","locator":"Part A, \"Safeguards Statement for 2022,\" paragraph covering the five nuclear-weapon states and ten selected facilities; Part B, China entry under voluntary-offer agreements","provisional_id":"WEB-1B-C1-011","canonical_fact_key":"iaea-limited-voluntary-safeguards-china-2022","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["1B"],"methodology_ids":["1B.3"],"source_type":"international_organization_audit_report","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Authoritative annual IAEA safeguards implementation record; its scope limitation is part of the formal safeguards framework.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Published later specifically to report safeguards implemented during calendar year 2022.","ruler_attribution":"National safeguards cooperation occurred under Xi’s administration, but facility selection and implementation are institutional and do not establish his personal action.","contrary_evidence":true,"underlying_fact_key":"iaea-china-voluntary-offer-safeguards-2022","lenses":["1B.3"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"2022 Joint Statement on the Comprehensive Nuclear-Test-Ban Treaty","publisher":"Comprehensive Nuclear-Test-Ban Treaty Organization","publication_date":"2022-09-21","url":"https://www.ctbto.org/sites/default/files/2022-09/2022_Joint_Statement_on_the_Comprehensive_Nuclear-Test-Ban_Treaty.pdf","claim":"The contemporaneous 2022 CTBT joint statement identified China among the eight Annex 2 states whose ratification was still required for treaty entry into force. This supplies a precise 2022 locator for continuing Chinese non-ratification, separate from China’s cooperation with monitoring stations.","locator":"Opening paragraph and footnote 2 listing the eight remaining Annex 2 states: China, DPRK, Egypt, India, Iran, Israel, Pakistan, and the United States","provisional_id":"WEB-1B-C1-012","canonical_fact_key":"china-ctbt-not-ratified-2022","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["1B"],"methodology_ids":["1B.3","1B.10"],"source_type":"international_organization_treaty_status_record","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous official CTBTO document with an explicit state list and precise treaty-status implication.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Dated September 2022 and directly establishes treaty status during the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Ratification remained incomplete during Xi’s rule, but the record does not establish that Xi personally blocked ratification.","contrary_evidence":true,"underlying_fact_key":"china-ctbt-not-ratified-2022","lenses":["1B.3","1B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Full text of the report to the 20th National Congress of the Communist Party of China","publisher":"State Council of the People’s Republic of China / Xinhua","publication_date":"2022-10-25","url":"https://english.www.gov.cn/2022special/20thcpccongress/","claim":"In his 2022 Party Congress report, Xi called for fully implementing the system of ultimate responsibility resting with the CMC chairman while strengthening strategic deterrence and accelerating weapons modernization. This is the separate primary-source locator previously bundled into WD001; it establishes Xi’s asserted command responsibility but not independent proof of implementation quality.","locator":"Document link \"Full text of the report to the 20th National Congress of the Communist Party of China\"; section XII, \"Achieving the Centenary Goal of the People’s Liberation Army and Further Modernizing National Defense and the Military,\" paragraphs on the CMC chairman responsibility system and strategic deterrence","provisional_id":"WEB-1B-C1-013","canonical_fact_key":"xi-2022-cmc-chairman-responsibility-report","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["1B"],"methodology_ids":["1B.1","1B.3","1B.7"],"source_type":"primary_official_speech","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Authenticated primary text delivered by Xi; authoritative for his stated directives, though not an independent implementation check.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Delivered and approved in October 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Direct: these are Xi’s own report and directives as Party general secretary and CMC chairman.","contrary_evidence":false,"underlying_fact_key":"xi-2022-cmc-chairman-responsibility-report","lenses":["1B.1","1B.3","1B.7"]}
